"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"][17]={"university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M17-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M17-L1 – Radiation, Near Field and Far Field<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Bachelor: Antennas<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish field regions,\ncalculate far-field quantities and describe linear polarisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a Transmission Line to Free Space<\/h2>\n<p>A transmission line guides electromagnetic energy along its geometry.\nAn antenna couples part of the power into freely propagating\nelectromagnetic waves. On reception, the coupling operates in the\nopposite direction. Time-varying charges and currents determine the\nfields; a static charge distribution alone does not produce continuous\nradiation. The static field, stored reactive energy and radiated energy\nare different aspects of the same field model.<\/p>\n<p>For a harmonic wave in homogeneous vacuum,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=1/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\epsilon_0}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=c/f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=2\\pi/\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor propagation in the positive z-direction, we can use RMS phasors to\nwrite<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒆<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><munder><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑯<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒆<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underline{\\mathbf E}=E_0e^{-jkz}\\mathbf e_x,\\qquad \\underline{\\mathbf H}=\\frac{E_0}{Z_0}e^{-jkz}\\mathbf e_y.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>377<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_0\\approx377\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the free-space wave impedance, not the antenna input impedance. The\ntime-averaged power flux density is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑺<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Re<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo>×<\/mo><msup><munder><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑯<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">_<\/mo><\/munder><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle\\mathbf S\\rangle=\\operatorname{Re}(\\underline{\\mathbf E}\\times\\underline{\\mathbf H}^{*})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_0^2/Z_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin W/m². With peak-value phasors, an additional factor of 1/2 would be\nrequired.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Distance Regions<\/h2>\n<p>The fields of a small harmonic electric dipole contain terms whose\nmagnitudes decrease as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/r^3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/r^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOnly at large distances does the radiation term\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndominate. For electrically large apertures, the variation in path\nlengths across the aperture is decisive. The Fraunhofer distance\nguideline is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>D<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_F\\approx\\frac{2D^2}{\\lambda}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the largest relevant dimension. The expression follows approximately\nfrom the quadratic path difference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><msup><mi>D<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta r\\approx D^2/(8r)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe corresponding maximum phase deviation relative to the aperture\ncentre is about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt is not a universal sharp boundary: the distance must also be\nsufficiently large relative to both the wavelength and the antenna size.\nA common guideline for the inner Fresnel boundary of electrically large\nantennas is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.62<\/mn><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>D<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.62\\sqrt{D^3/\\lambda}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOther criteria are appropriate for small dipoles. In the reactive near\nfield, the E/H ratio is not generally\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\neven in the radiating near field, the angular distribution may still\ndepend on distance.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_062_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic field regions around an aperture on the left, orthogonal E and H fields of a plane wave on the right.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Field regions around a finite aperture are\ndelineated schematically; in the plane far-field wave model, electric\nfield, magnetic field and propagation direction are mutually\nperpendicular.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Polarisation and Spherical Waves<\/h2>\n<p>Polarisation describes the trajectory of the electric field vector\nover time at a fixed location. In-phase orthogonal components give\nlinear polarisation; equal magnitudes and a 90° phase difference give\ncircular polarisation. Otherwise, the general case is an ellipse. For\ncircular polarisation, the viewing direction must be specified to define\nthe sense of rotation.<\/p>\n<p>In the far field, power propagates approximately radially. For\nisotropic radiation,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=P_\\mathrm{rad}/(4\\pi r^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA directional source changes the angular distribution, not the total\npower. For a fixed angular interval, the flux density decreases with the\nsquare of the distance, while the field amplitude decreases\napproximately as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nReflections or obstacles can alter this relationship locally.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Model Limitations<\/h2>\n<p>At 2.4 GHz,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1249<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.1249<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nm. An aperture of 0.8 m has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>10.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_F\\approx10.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nm and an estimated inner Fresnel boundary of 1.26 m. At 1 km, the\ndistance for this idealised geometry is considerably greater than both.\nAn isotropic source with 0.01 W of radiated power produces\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>7.96<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S\\approx7.96\\cdot10^{-10}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nW/m² and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.548<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\mathrm{eff}\\approx0.548<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV/m there. Here the original subscript eff denotes the RMS value. This\ncalculation is neither a measurement nor a statement about exposure\nlimits. At 0.5 m, however, a simple far-field model would be unsuitable\nfor this aperture.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What wavelength results at 900 MHz? <strong>Solution:<\/strong>\napproximately 0.333 m.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Double D while keeping the frequency unchanged.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> the Fraunhofer distance guideline increases\nby a factor of four.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to the isotropic power flux at three times the\ndistance? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> it falls to 1/9, and the field\namplitude to 1/3.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is H at 1 V/m RMS in a plane free-space field?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>377<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.65<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/377\\approx2.65<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmA/m.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://opencourses.emu.edu.tr/course/view.php?id=395\">Eastern\nMediterranean University – EENG463 Antenna Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Overview of\nElectromagnetics and Fundamental Parameters of Antennas: radiation and\nfield quantities. Course outline, PDFs not reviewed. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://vtechworks.lib.vt.edu/bitstream/handle/10919/93253/Electromagnetics_Vol2_screen-reader-friendly.pdf\">Steven\nW. Ellingson – Electromagnetics, Volume 2 (2020, VT Publishing, PDF)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· §§ 9.4–9.7 and 10.7: radiation and far-field fundamentals; not a\ncomplete reference for all near-field boundaries. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M17-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M17-L2 – Antenna Parameters and Radiation Patterns<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Bachelor: Antennas<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Integrate a radiation pattern,\ndistinguish gain from directivity and use dBi correctly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Direction-Dependent Power Radiation<\/h2>\n<p>In the far field, the radiation intensity is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U(\\theta,\\phi)=r^2 S_r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin W/sr. Integrating over solid angle gives the radiated power:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msubsup><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\mathrm{rad}=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi U(\\theta,\\phi)\\sin\\theta\\,d\\theta\\,d\\phi.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the polar angle relative to the z-axis, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϕ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the azimuth. The weighting factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust not be omitted, because an angular rectangle near a pole covers\nless solid angle than one at the equator. For a normalised power pattern\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=U/U_\\mathrm{max}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe beam solid angle is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega_A=\\int F\\,d\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe maximum directivity is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_0=4\\pi/\\Omega_A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDirectivity is dimensionless; it compares the intensity in a given\ndirection with the spatial average for the same radiated power.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Gain, Efficiency and Reference Power<\/h2>\n<p>We distinguish the power accepted at the antenna terminal,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfrom the power actually radiated. With radiation efficiency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=P_\\mathrm{rad}/P_a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G(\\theta,\\phi)=\\eta D(\\theta,\\phi),\\qquad G_\\mathrm{dBi}=10\\log_{10}G.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Realised gain additionally accounts for mismatch at the specified\ninput; this must not be deducted a second time in a link budget.\nFeed-line losses are accounted for separately unless the reference plane\nfor the data-sheet gain is already upstream of the line. dBi refers to\nan ideal isotropic radiator. dBd refers to an ideal half-wave dipole in\nits direction of maximum radiation (approximately 2.15 dBi). A passive\nsystem generates no additional energy: its directional gain results from\nredistribution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reading a Radiation Pattern<\/h2>\n<p>An electrically short z-oriented dipole has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(\\theta)=\\sin^2\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe maximum is at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith nulls along the axis. A cut in a plane containing the dipole axis\nforms two opposing lobes; the azimuthal cut at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=90^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis circular. The three-dimensional pattern is not spherical. The\nhalf-power points of a main lobe are at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>45<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">45^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>135<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">135^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving a half-power beamwidth of 90°. In dB, the half-power points are\nat approximately −3.01 dB. A field-amplitude ratio of 0.5, by contrast,\ncorresponds to −6.02 dB.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_063_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Normalised dipole power pattern in polar and Cartesian representations with half-power points.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The normalized dipole pattern is shown in Cartesian\nand polar form; separation of the half-power points gives a 90°\nbeamwidth in the illustrated plane.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>For a directional antenna, sidelobes, nulls and the front-to-back\nratio are also relevant. A single half-power beamwidth cannot replace a\nthree-dimensional pattern, and a narrow beam in only one plane does not\nguarantee high directivity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Integration and Numerical Example<\/h2>\n<p>For the short dipole, analytical integration gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/msubsup><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mi>θ<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>3<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega_A=2\\pi\\int_0^\\pi\\sin^3\\theta\\,d\\theta=\\frac{8\\pi}{3},\\qquad D_0=\\frac32.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The directivity is therefore 1.76 dBi. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe maximum gain is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor 0.79 dBi. If 5 W is accepted, 4 W is radiated and 1 W is dissipated.\nThe maximum radiation intensity is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4775<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\cdot1.5/(4\\pi)=0.4775<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nW/sr. For a receiver at a distance of 100 m, this gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>47.75<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">47.75\\,\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>W/m²\nin that direction. For an independent numerical check, integrate the\nnormalised dipole pattern with the solid-angle weight using 90, 180 and\n360 equal polar-angle intervals (midpoints); check convergence towards\nthe analytical directivity of 1.5. The efficiency is a chosen model\nparameter, not a typical measured value for arbitrary short dipoles.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is 10 dBi as a linear ratio? <strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n10.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_0=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_0=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\napproximately 3.01 dBi.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is an azimuthal cut of a vertical dipole described as\nomnidirectional? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> at a fixed polar angle, it\nis independent of azimuth; it is not the same in all spatial\ndirections.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A pattern has half-power points at 20° and 50°.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> a half-power beamwidth of 30° in this\ncut.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://opencourses.emu.edu.tr/course/view.php?id=395\">Eastern\nMediterranean University – EENG463 Antenna Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Fundamental\nParameters of Antennas-1: radiation pattern, gain and polarization.\nCourse outline, PDFs not reviewed. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://vtechworks.lib.vt.edu/bitstream/handle/10919/93253/Electromagnetics_Vol2_screen-reader-friendly.pdf\">Steven\nW. Ellingson – Electromagnetics, Volume 2 (2020, VT Publishing, PDF)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· §§ 10.5, 10.7–10.8, pp. 173–182: radiation efficiency, directivity,\ngain and radiation pattern. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M17-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M17-L3 – Dipoles, Apertures and Arrays<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Bachelor: Antennas<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L2<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compare ideal antenna types and\nderive the array factor for two radiators.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Dipole Models<\/h2>\n<p>For the ideal short (Hertzian) dipole, a spatially constant current\nover a very short length is assumed. Its far-field magnitude is\nproportional to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA physical thin, short, centre-fed wire, by contrast, has an\napproximately triangular current distribution; in particular, its\nradiation resistance must therefore not be equated with that of the\nHertzian current element.<\/p>\n<p>For the thin half-wave dipole, we use an approximately sinusoidal\ncurrent distribution. The normalised far-field amplitude is then\ndescribed by<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(\\theta)=\\frac{\\cos[(\\pi/2)\\cos\\theta]}{\\sin\\theta}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>with a limiting value of zero at both poles. The normalised power\nvalue is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|A|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe ideal thin half-wave dipole has approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.64<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_0=1.64<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(2.15 dBi). Its physical resonant length depends on the wire diameter\nand surroundings. A geometrically exact half-wavelength does not\ngenerally ensure a purely real input impedance or a match.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Aperture and Effective Area<\/h2>\n<p>The effective receiving area of a reciprocal antenna under conjugate\nmatching and matched polarisation is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>G<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_e=\\frac{\\lambda^2 G}{4\\pi}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>It is a measure of power coupling, not a direct measure of the\ngeometric silhouette. For a large aperture of area\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_\\mathrm{geom}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe often write\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_e=\\eta_a A_\\mathrm{geom}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith aperture efficiency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis relationship is useful for typical aperture antennas but should not\nbe interpreted as a geometric limit for every wire radiator. A larger\neffective area allows greater gain at the same frequency; with typical\naperture illumination, the main lobe narrows. The actual field\ndistribution also affects sidelobes and efficiency.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two Elements and Relative Phase<\/h2>\n<p>Two equally strong ideal isotropic model sources lie on the z-axis at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=\\pm d/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith additional feed phase\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>β<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat the positive element, we use the far-field convention<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">AF(\\theta)=1+e^{j(kd\\cos\\theta+\\beta)},\\quad F_n=\\frac{|AF|^2}{4}=\\cos^2\\left(\\frac{kd\\cos\\theta+\\beta}{2}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The common spatial phase has been omitted.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis normalised to the theoretical peak value of the sum; not every\narbitrary combination of d and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>β<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nreaches this value within the visible angular range. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=\\lambda/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe maximum is broadside to the axis at 90°, and the nulls are at the\npoles. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe maximum is at the poles and the null is broadside. The model assumes\nprescribed currents, no mutual coupling and identical element patterns.\nReal dipoles may nevertheless be unable to serve a direction intended by\nthe array factor because of their own null in that direction.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_064_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Normalised dipole and array power patterns for two isotropic sources at half-wavelength spacing and two relative phases.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Dipole models and an array of two ideal radiators\nproduce different directional patterns; with half-wavelength spacing,\nrelative phase shifts the main lobes.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Design Limitations<\/h2>\n<p>At 2.4 GHz, an idealised half-wavelength spacing is approximately\n6.25 cm. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>60<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=60^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">kd\\cos\\theta=\\pi/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_n=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor an in-phase two-element array with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=\\lambda/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nintegrating the normalised power pattern gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega_A=2\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_0=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(3.01 dBi). This is not 6 dBi, although the field sum at the maximum is\ntwice that of an equally excited single element and the local power\ndensity is four times as high: the total power is doubled under the\nchosen ideal geometry.<\/p>\n<p>For an area of 0.1 m² and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_a=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nat 2.4 GHz we obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>48.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G\\approx48.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor approximately 16.84 dBi. This simplified aperture value cannot\nreplace electromagnetic simulation or measurement of impedance or\nradiation pattern. Larger array spacings can produce additional equally\nstrong maxima; practical feed networks introduce losses and phase\nerrors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At 2.4 GHz, determine the idealised half-wavelength.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> approximately 6.25 cm.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=\\lambda/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=90^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_n=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Same geometry,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=90^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_n=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does 6 dB higher local power not automatically mean 6 dBi\ndirectivity? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> the total radiated power and its\nangular distribution must also be taken into account.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://opencourses.emu.edu.tr/course/view.php?id=395\">Eastern\nMediterranean University – EENG463 Antenna Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Linear Wire\nAntennas, Linear Arrays and Aperture Antennas: dipoles, arrays and\napertures. Course outline, PDFs not reviewed. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://vtechworks.lib.vt.edu/bitstream/handle/10919/93253/Electromagnetics_Vol2_screen-reader-friendly.pdf\">Steven\nW. Ellingson – Electromagnetics, Volume 2 (2020, VT Publishing, PDF)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· §§ 9.5, 9.7 and 10.13: dipole radiation and effective aperture; this\nreference does not establish the array factor. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M17-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M17-L4 – Matching and Propagation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Bachelor: Antennas<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L2<\/a>,\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L6<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate reflection,\npolarisation losses and free-space path loss separately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Connection and Reflection<\/h2>\n<p>An antenna has a complex input impedance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_A=R_A+jX_A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat its feed point. On a lossless transmission line with real reference\nimpedance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma=\\frac{Z_A-Z_c}{Z_A+Z_c},\\quad P_a=P_+(1-|\\Gamma|^2).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_+<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the forward power at the reference point. The standing wave\nratio is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">SWR=(1+|\\Gamma|)/(1-|\\Gamma|)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the positive return loss is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>20<\/mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">RL=-20\\log_{10}|\\Gamma|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn measurements, by contrast,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>11<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_{11}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis often displayed as a negative dB value. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_A=100\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_c=50\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma=1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nSWR 2 and RL 9.54 dB. 11.11% of the forward power is reflected, and the\nmismatch loss is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.512<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-10\\log_{10}(8/9)=0.512<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndB. A well-matched antenna may nevertheless be inefficient: a\nterminating resistor can be perfectly matched while converting most of\nthe power into heat.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reception and Polarisation<\/h2>\n<p>With matched polarisation and conjugate load matching, the incident\npower flux density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the available received power\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_r=S A_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor two linearly polarised antennas with an angular offset\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ψ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\psi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbetween their polarisation directions (along the same propagation axis),\nthe additional power factor is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>ψ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=\\cos^2\\psi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis factor concerns the field projection, not necessarily the geometric\nradiation pattern. At 45°, the loss is 3.01 dB; at 60°, it is 6.02 dB.\nAt 90°, the ideal model has a null; real antennas and multipath\npropagation may produce finite coupling.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Friis Equation<\/h2>\n<p>Combining\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=P_tG_t/(4\\pi r^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_e=\\lambda^2G_r/(4\\pi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives, in free space,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_r=P_t G_t G_r\\left(\\frac{\\lambda}{4\\pi r}\\right)^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the accepted transmit power and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the available received power with matched polarisation. The\nfree-space path loss is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_\\mathrm{FS}=20\\log_{10}(4\\pi r/\\lambda)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin dB. With distance in km and frequency in MHz, the approximate\nexpression is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>32.45<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_\\mathrm{FS}=32.45+20\\log_{10}r_\\mathrm{km}+20\\log_{10}f_\\mathrm{MHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt 2.4 GHz and 1 km, the loss is 100.05 dB. Doubling the distance\nincreases the loss by 6.02 dB. Doubling the frequency also increases the\nisotropic free-space path loss by 6.02 dB; with constant physical\nreceiving apertures, however, the gain may change at the same time. A\ncomparison must therefore specify which quantities are held\nconstant.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_065_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Reflected power fraction for real loads, polarisation loss and free-space path loss at two frequencies.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Mismatch at a real load, polarization misalignment\nand free-space path loss affect a link budget through different\nmechanisms and should not be treated as the same loss\nfactor.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Terrestrial Propagation and the Fresnel Zone<\/h2>\n<p>For the first Fresnel zone, the approximate radius is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_1=\\sqrt{\\lambda d_1d_2/(d_1+d_2)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor two path segments of 500 m each at 2.4 GHz, the radius is 5.59 m.\nObstacles within this zone may affect received power even with a\ngeometric line of sight. The Fresnel zone must be distinguished from the\nFresnel near-field region of an individual antenna. Ground reflections,\nvegetation, buildings, Earth’s curvature and atmospheric refraction\nalter actual transmission. Free-space path loss is therefore a\nreference, not a guarantee of a given field strength or availability. A\nmultipath channel can produce both constructive and destructive\ninterference. A fixed additional attenuation is only a sensitivity\nscenario, not a physically derived multipath channel.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_A=25\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_c=50\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma=-1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nSWR 2, 11.11% reflected power.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What attenuation results from a 30° polarisation offset?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-10\\log_{10}(0.75)=1.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndB.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Increase r tenfold at the same frequency. <strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n+20 dB free-space path loss.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is an unobstructed line of sight sufficient for the Friis model?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> No, multipath effects and obstacles within\nthe Fresnel zone may alter the calculation.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://opencourses.emu.edu.tr/course/view.php?id=395\">Eastern\nMediterranean University – EENG463 Antenna Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Fundamental\nParameters of Antennas-1/-2: input impedance, effective aperture and\nFriis equation. Course outline, PDFs not reviewed. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.itu.int/rec/R-REC-P.525-5-202411-I/en\">ITU-R\nP.525-5 – Calculation of free-space attenuation (11/2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · ITU-R\nP.525-5 (11/2024), annex §2.3, p. 3, eqs. (5)–(6): free-space\nattenuation; PDF text retrieved, page image unavailable. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
